1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,552
My smart mirror just lied to my face, or maybe it finally told me the brutal truth.

2
00:00:06,852 --> 00:00:18,348
Every morning, Elena, 28, messy dark hair, grey oversized sweater, sees her reflection here. Today, the glass shows text instead.

3
00:00:18,648 --> 00:00:27,552
It displays raw thoughts. Not mine. My husband Mark's. The screen claims he regrets every single year we spent together.

4
00:00:27,852 --> 00:00:38,268
Panic tightens my chest. I run downstairs, finding Mark in the kitchen drinking coffee, smiling his usual warm, gentle morning smile.

5
00:00:38,568 --> 00:00:47,448
He kisses my forehead, asking if I slept well. His eyes hold pure affection. But the mirror upstairs said he hates me.

6
00:00:47,748 --> 00:00:56,820
Confused and desperate for answers, I rush back upstairs to watch the glass. Maybe it's glitching. Maybe it's all a cruel joke.

7
00:00:57,120 --> 00:01:06,672
The text updates instantly. New words scroll past: 'She's finally gone downstairs. Time to breathe.' But Mark is still down there.

8
00:01:06,972 --> 00:01:14,508
A cold dread washes over me. The thoughts aren't coming from Mark at all. They are broadcasting from someone much closer.

9
00:01:14,808 --> 00:01:23,616
Heavy footsteps echo down the upstairs hallway. A shadow falls across the doorframe. The guest living in our spare room.

10
00:01:23,916 --> 00:01:29,028
If he isn't thinking about my husband, then whose life am I actually living right now?
